Find a scoped variable through the variable hash table.

resolve_scope_var walks a chain of every lexical variable visible from
a scope, inner scopes first and newest first within a scope, and stops
at the first with the name it resolves; it walks the chain of each
enclosing function the same way. In the large functions of bundled
code that was 15% of the compile time of GitHub's code view module
(3.7 million steps for 100,000 lookups).

When a function has the variable hash table (27 variables or more),
scope_chain_lookup now enumerates the variables of that name from the
table and picks the one the walk would stop at: the one in the
innermost scope of the chain, the newest in that scope. It falls back
to the walk for a function with a 'with' scope or parameter
expressions, for 'arguments', and before the chains are linked
(linked_var_count). The bytecode is unchanged (GitHub's 77 modules,
Aura's tests and QuickJS's tests compile to the same bytes; a build
that walked as well and compared found no difference). Compiling the
77 modules took 245 ms instead of 256 ms on the host.

